```{r setup}
library(sharepointr)
```

Most sharepointr functions accept a SharePoint URL in place of a site, drive, list, or item object. This makes it possible to use a link copied from SharePoint (using the "Copy link" option described in Microsoft's guide to [sharing SharePoint files or folders](https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/share-sharepoint-files-or-folders-1fe37332-0f9a-4719-970e-d2578da4941c)) or from your browser address bar.

This is a folder URL. It is a shared link where people with existing access can use the link.

```{r}
dir_url <- "https://bmore.sharepoint.com/:f:/r/sites/DOP-CPR/Shared%20Documents/INSPIRE%20Program%20%F0%9F%8F%AB%F0%9F%9A%B8%F0%9F%8C%B3?csf=1&web=1&e=W5dbYz"
```

SharePoint uses many types of URLs and they don't all work with this package yet. Use `sp_url_parse()` to see how a URL is parsed into smaller parts:

```{r}
sp_url_parse(dir_url)
```

These parts are used to get the site, drive (document library), list, or item from SharePoint:

```{r}
get_sp_site(dir_url)

get_sp_drive(dir_url)
```

With a directory URL, you can get a list of files:

```{r}
dir_info <- sp_dir_info(dir_url)

dir_info
```

You can use the same URL to get a character vector of file names:

```{r}
sp_dir_ls(dir_url)
```

But you can't use that URL to get a list:

```{r, error=TRUE}
get_sp_list(dir_url)
```

SharePoint list URLs come in a few different styles and they all work. A shared link for a list includes `:l:`:

```{r}
list_url <- "https://bmore.sharepoint.com/:l:/r/sites/MayorsOffice-DataGovernance/Lists/Data%20Governance%20Progress%20Tracker?e=0P1fFX"

get_sp_list(list_url)
```

The URL in your browser address bar when viewing a list includes the name of the list view page (e.g. `AllItems.aspx`) and may include the query parameter `env=WebViewList`. A URL with the list name but no view page also works:

```{r}
list_view_url <- "https://bmore.sharepoint.com/sites/MayorsOffice-DataGovernance/Lists/Data%20Governance%20Progress%20Tracker/AllItems.aspx?env=WebViewList"

sp_url_parse(list_view_url)[c("site_url", "list_name")]

sp_url_parse(
  "https://bmore.sharepoint.com/sites/MayorsOffice-DataGovernance/Lists/Data%20Governance%20Progress%20Tracker"
)[c("site_url", "list_name")]
```

Shared URLs for files work with `get_sp_item()` and `get_sp_item_properties()`:

```{r}
file_url <- "https://bmore.sharepoint.com/:w:/r/sites/DOP-CPR/Shared%20Documents/INSPIRE%20Program%20%F0%9F%8F%AB%F0%9F%9A%B8%F0%9F%8C%B3/INSPIRE%20Plans%20Adopted%20%E2%9C%85/Govans%20ES/Govans%20Final%20Planning%20Document%20and%20related%20materials/Govans%20INSPIRE%20Plan%20-%20Final%20draft%20before%20layout.docx?d=w3408fbc67fa2462187409eb240eb4df2&csf=1&web=1&e=4dcgze"

get_sp_item(file_url)
```

A shared link like this one includes the path to the file, so it stops working if the file is moved or renamed. A document URL (the URL in your browser address bar when a file is open in Word, Excel, or PowerPoint online) uses the item id instead, so it keeps working after a file is moved. It works as long as the default library is set to match the document library of the supplied URL:

```{r}
document_url <- "https://bmore.sharepoint.com/:w:/r/sites/DOP-CPR/_layouts/15/Doc.aspx?sourcedoc=%7B3408FBC6-7FA2-4621-8740-9EB240EB4DF2%7D&file=Govans%20INSPIRE%20Plan%20-%20Final%20draft%20before%20layout.docx&action=default&mobileredirect=true"

get_sp_item(document_url)
```

The `is_sp_url()` family of functions (e.g. `is_sp_folder_url()`) can be used to check what type of URL you have before using it.
